# Historical findings that are provably not secrets.
#
# Same policy as .gitleaks.toml's allowlist: entries must be provably
# non-secret, scoped as narrowly as possible, and justified here. Broad path or
# rule exclusions are not accepted — and neither is the 3-field GLOBAL form
# (`file:rule-id:start-line`), which gitleaks checks FIRST and which matches in
# every commit, so it would silently suppress a genuine secret re-introduced at
# the same path and rule. Use the 4-field commit form only:
#
#   commit:file:rule-id:start-line
#
# A wrong entry can only leave the scan RED (the fingerprint is a map key that
# is never hit, and the finding is still reported). There is no way for a wrong
# entry to turn the scan green, which is the property that makes this file
# reviewable at all.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL: gitleaks-action scans only the pushed commits on
# `push`, but the whole history on `schedule`. Any secret ever committed is
# immortal, so a remediated tree does not make the scheduled scan pass — an
# entry here does. Keeping it green is the point: a red gate is one where the
# NEXT finding is invisible, which is how a real leak would be missed.

